---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import { projects, site } from "../../data/site";
import Cta from "../../components/Cta.astro";
import DataRows from "../../components/DataRows.astro";
import Flow from "../../components/Flow.astro";
import Manifest from "../../components/Manifest.astro";
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";

const monark = projects[0];

const toc = [
  { id: "problem", label: "01  THE PROBLEM" },
  { id: "architecture", label: "02  ARCHITECTURE" },
  { id: "data", label: "03  DATA MODEL" },
  { id: "stack", label: "04  STACK" },
];

const meta = [
  { k: "PRODUCT", v: "Monark" },
  { k: "ROLE", v: "Everything" },
  { k: "STACK", v: "Next.js · TS" },
  { k: "DATA", v: "Postgres · Prisma" },
  { k: "HOST", v: "Vercel" },
];

// Left to right: where a write goes and what it crosses on the way.
const flow = [
  { name: "CLIENT", note: "next.js", lit: true },
  { name: "SERVER ACTIONS", note: "user-scoped" },
  { name: "PRISMA", note: "typed" },
  { name: "POSTGRES", note: "source of truth", lit: true },
];

const tables = [
  [
    "transactions",
    "Amounts in minor units as bigint, with an income or expense type rather than a sign. Category is a foreign key, never a string.",
  ],
  [
    "budgets",
    "Unique on user, category and month, so two budgets cannot fight over the same month and the database is what says so.",
  ],
  [
    "recurring_transactions",
    "A template plus the last month it was advanced past, so confirming or skipping a month is idempotent and a missed run cannot double-post.",
  ],
  [
    "goals",
    "Target plus optional deadline. Progress is summed from contributions, never stored, because stored progress goes stale silently.",
  ],
];

const manifest = [
  { label: "CLIENT", items: "Next.js · React · TypeScript · Tailwind" },
  { label: "SERVER", items: "Node · Server Actions · NextAuth · bcrypt" },
  { label: "DATA", items: "Postgres · Prisma · pg" },
  { label: "SERVICES", items: "Vercel · Resend · Gemini" },
];
---

<Doc
  title={`Monark case study · ${site.name}`}
  description="How Monark was built: why the dashboard opens with a sentence instead of a chart, why every total is derived rather than stored, and why money is a branded integer."
  ogType="article"
  label="CASE STUDY 01 / MONARK"
  back={{ href: "/work", label: "WORK INDEX" }}
  meta={meta}
  tocTitle="Contents"
  toc={toc}
>
  <Fragment slot="headline">
    <div class="eyebrow mb-[26px]">OWN PRODUCT &nbsp;·&nbsp; IN PRODUCTION &nbsp;·&nbsp; 2026</div>
    <h1
      class="max-w-[820px] text-[clamp(44px,6vw,84px)] leading-[0.96] font-medium tracking-[-0.04em]"
    >
      <span class="line"><span data-line class="block">A finance app that</span></span>
      <span class="line"
        ><span data-line class="block"><span class="text-mint">forecasts</span> instead of</span
        ></span
      >
      <span class="line"><span data-line class="block">reporting.</span></span>
    </h1>
  </Fragment>

  <div data-mask slot="banner" class="overflow-hidden border-b border-line bg-panel">
    <Image
      src={monark.shot}
      alt="Monark dashboard"
      widths={[900, 1400, 1900]}
      sizes="100vw"
      class="block h-[520px] w-full object-cover object-left-top"
    />
  </div>

  <section id="problem" class="scroll-mt-[90px]">
    <div class="eyebrow mb-5">01&nbsp;&nbsp;THE PROBLEM</div>
    <h2 data-reveal class="text-[34px] leading-[1.12] font-medium tracking-[-0.025em]">
      Budget apps report the past. Nobody needs a report.
    </h2>
    <p data-reveal class="mt-[22px] text-lg leading-[1.65] text-mute">
      Every personal finance tool I tried answered the wrong question. They tell you what you spent,
      in a chart, after the fact. The question that actually matters on day four of the month is
      whether you are on track to end it badly.
    </p>
    <p data-reveal class="mt-[18px] text-lg leading-[1.65] text-mute">
      So Monark's dashboard does not open with a chart. It opens with a sentence: your net position
      and where the month is pacing to. The visualisation is secondary to the statement.
    </p>
    <div data-reveal class="mt-7 border-l-2 border-mint bg-mint/5 py-3.5 pl-5">
      <div class="text-[17px] leading-[1.6] text-paper">
        The design constraint I set: if the headline needs a legend to be understood, it is wrong.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="architecture" class="mt-[70px] scroll-mt-[90px]">
    <div class="eyebrow mb-5">02&nbsp;&nbsp;ARCHITECTURE</div>
    <h2 data-reveal class="text-[34px] leading-[1.12] font-medium tracking-[-0.025em]">
      Deliberately small.
    </h2>
    <p data-reveal class="mt-[22px] text-lg leading-[1.65] text-mute">
      One Next.js app, one Postgres database, Prisma between them. No microservices, no message bus,
      no event sourcing. A single-user finance app does not need distributed anything, and every
      extra moving part is something that can be down at 2am for no revenue.
    </p>

    <Flow fig="FIG. 02.1 / SYSTEM" nodes={flow} notes={["EVERY TOTAL IS DERIVED ON READ, NOTHING IS CACHED OR ROLLED UP","NO QUEUE, NO CRON, NO SECOND COPY OF A NUMBER"]} />
  </section>

  <section id="data" class="mt-[70px] scroll-mt-[90px]">
    <div class="eyebrow mb-5">03&nbsp;&nbsp;DATA MODEL</div>
    <h2 data-reveal class="text-[34px] leading-[1.12] font-medium tracking-[-0.025em]">
      Constraints in the schema, not in the app.
    </h2>
    <p data-reveal class="mt-[22px] text-lg leading-[1.65] text-mute">
      Anything the database can guarantee, the database guarantees. Application code is where rules
      go to be forgotten.
    </p>

    <DataRows rows={tables} />
  </section>

  <section id="stack" class="mt-[70px] scroll-mt-[90px]">
    <div class="eyebrow mb-5">04&nbsp;&nbsp;STACK MANIFEST</div>
    <Manifest rows={manifest} />
  </section>

  <Cta heading="Want this level of thinking on your product?" layout="inline" />
</Doc>
